## Introduction to Jupyter AI

Jupyter AI brings the power of large language models directly into your Jupyter notebook, making it easy to interact with AI as part of your workflow. Prompting in Jupyter AI is simple—just use the special `%%sdo_llm` command at the top of a cell, followed by your prompt (question or instruction):
### Interacting with an LLM within the notebook

The following loads the LLM:
<img src="materials/images/cell_magic_load.png"/>
### ⚠️ Setup: Load the sdo_llm_magic Extension (Run First)
Make sure to run the cell below before using any %%sdo_llm commands.
🟢 ▶ Run the cells below to continue.
%load_ext sdo_llm_magic
#### Once loaded, you can interact with the LLM by prefixing any cell with **`%%sdo_llm`**. The cell’s contents will be sent to the model and the response will be displayed below.
<img src="materials/images/cell_magic.png"/>
### Example 1: Basic Output (No Formatting Instructions)
🟢 ▶ Run the cell below to continue.
%%sdo_llm
If my dataset has multiple modes, what does that mean and how should I interpret it?
### Example 2: Markdown Output (Notebook-Friendly)
If the results are in Markdown and you’d like to make them look nicer, you can load the following library:
🟢 ▶ Run the cells below to continue.
from IPython.display import display, Markdown
%%sdo_llm
display(Markdown(If my dataset has multiple modes, what does that mean and how should I interpret it?))
### Example 3: Plain Text Output (Patient-Report Style)
Organizing LLM Output into Structured Sections (Plain Text Mode)
🟢 ▶ Run the cell below to continue.
%%sdo_llm
If my dataset has multiple modes, what does that mean and how should I interpret it?

Write in well-formatted plain text.
Do NOT use markdown symbols (**, #, -, etc.).
Use:
- Clear spacing
- Line breaks between sections
- Simple numbered headings (1., 2., 3.)

**The model will respond right in the notebook, just like a conversation.**
<br>
_It’s like having a research assistant built into your workflow._
